.TH TULPAR-REMOVE 1 "2026" "tulpar 0.1.0" "Tulpar Manual"
.SH NAME
tulpar-remove, tulpar-uninstall \- remove an installed package
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tulpar remove
.RI [ options "] " package " [" package "...]"
.br
.B tulpar uninstall
.RI [ options "] " package " [" package "...]"
.br
.B tulpar r
.RI [ options "] " package " [" package "...]"
.br
.B tulpar \-r
.RI [ options "] " package " [" package "...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Removes one or more installed packages.
.B remove
and
.B uninstall
are equivalent spellings of the same command.
.PP
Removal of a package with other installed packages depending on it is
blocked; the dependents are printed so the conflict can be resolved
manually. Held packages (see
.BR tulpar-hold (1))
are likewise blocked from removal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dest " " \fIpath\fR
Remove from an alternate root instead of the live system.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, non-zero if the removal is blocked or the transaction
fails.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tulpar (1),
.BR tulpar-orphans (1),
.BR tulpar-hold (1)
